## About this Devlog

This devlog contains reports about progress in the project. It does not cover
everything that has happened, but it's a heartbeat that shows how the project
progresses over time.

This page lists entries for the year 2024, for future entries consult the [devlog archive](/devlog/).

## Entries

> []($block.attrs('note'))
> All of these posts were pulled from the [Discord](/community) server and were converted into the
> devlog in March 2026.

### [OpenSCAD]($section.id('2024-12-29T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

I've started sketching out a design with actual sizes in OpenSCAD to see if the desired
components actually fit into the case:

> []($block.attrs('pictures','large'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-12-29_express1.webp').linked(true))

### [Christmas Crafting Days!]($section.id('2024-12-25T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

I create a cardboard prototype of what i envision the computer to look like. it's slightly too short on the top-right dimension, but apart from that i'm pretty happy with the overall layout.

Modules left to right:
- Mainboard with display, two buttons, USB-C for programming/debugging and USB-A for devices
- High-end video card with HDMI, VGA and RCA/CVBS
- Stereo audio in/out with audio on pairs of RCA
- 4-port USB card
- 2-port RS-232
- 2-port C64 serial
- DIY board with a perfboard inside
- Digital and analog I/O on pin headers

> []($block.attrs('pictures','large'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-12-25-cardboard.webp').linked(true))


> []($block.attrs('note','throwback'))
> I wasn't aware how good of a design this was, and now, two years later, it still holds.
>
> The final design doesn't derive much from the christmas vision at all.

### [Brainstorming a case design]($section.id('2024-11-21T00:00:00').data('author','xq'))

In the last weeks, not much coding was going on, but i've sketched out the case for the home computer:

> []($block.attrs('pictures'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case1.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case2.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case3.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case4.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case5.webp').linked(true))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-11-21-case6.webp').linked(true))

The mug is the [IKEA standard mug](https://www.ikea.com/de/en/p/vaerdera-mug-white-10277366/), for size reference. The computer will get 7 expansion slots, one which can do video, and one which can do audio.

> []($block.attrs('note','throwback'))
> In hindsight, it's fun to remember that the computer originally was planned vertically like
> a typical PC system.
>
> Also the merge of the dedicated audio/video slots into the first two generic slots
> happened in the first two months of the design.
>
> Back then, the system still was intended to use dedicated USB, I²C and SPI per slot
> instead of using the Propeller 2 as a general purpose interface.

### [The beginning of a new era]($section.id("2024-09-05T00:00:00").data('author','xq'))

I was tinkering on the idea of the Ashet Home Computer for a long time, but with the [announcement of the Raspberry Pi Pico 2](https://www.raspberrypi.com/news/raspberry-pi-pico-2-our-new-5-microcontroller-board-on-sale-now/), the project suddenly got a real, exciting chance to get reality.

The RP2350 totally hit my desired target specs:

- 150 MHz RISC-V CPU
- Support for up to 16 MB of RAM
- Video output through PIO and HSTX

One thing led to another and after cooking on the idea for over a month in my head, i've started tinkering.

The **Ashet Home Computer™, Mark 4** design idea was born.

This is a rough sketch of the mainboard, and it's all built around the absent [RP2350 Stamp XL](https://www.solder.party/docs/rp2350-stamp-xl/), which should be possible to clip into the large hole in the middle:

> []($block.attrs('pictures','max'))
> []($image.siteAsset('img/devlog/2024/2024-09-05_a-new-beginning.webp').linked(true))

The initial design featured 9 expansion ports:

- 1 dedicated video expansion card port
- 1 dedicated audio expansion card port
- 7 regular expansion card ports

It also had mountable slots for flash and RAM chips.

Shout-out to _Arturo_ from [Solder Party](https://www.solder.party/) for coming up so quickly with the Stamp XL design!
